  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🎲 Baldur's Gate 3 Name Generator",
      "tagline": "A name for every playable race in BG3, built on each race's D&D naming customs."
    },
    "intro": "Baldur's Gate 3 is built on fifth-edition D&D, so each of its races brings its own naming customs: githyanki names like Lae'zel, dragonborn who put their clan first, tieflings with infernal or virtue names. This **Baldur's Gate 3 name generator** covers all eleven playable races, so your Tav sounds like they belong in the Sword Coast.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How names work for each BG3 race",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Human:</strong> a first name and a family name, in the Sword Coast's medieval style.</li><li><strong>Elf and Half-Elf:</strong> flowing elven names; half-elves often mix an elven first name with a human surname.</li><li><strong>Drow:</strong> a personal name and the noble house they belong to, like Minthara Baenre.</li><li><strong>Dwarf:</strong> a short, hard first name and a clan name, often about the forge or the deep.</li><li><strong>Halfling:</strong> a homely name and a family name that started as a nickname.</li><li><strong>Gnome:</strong> a bouncy personal name and a clan name.</li><li><strong>Half-Orc:</strong> a single strong name, as half-orcs often go by.</li><li><strong>Tiefling:</strong> an infernal name, or a virtue name like Hope or Torment.</li><li><strong>Githyanki:</strong> clipped names split by an apostrophe, like Lae'zel.</li><li><strong>Dragonborn:</strong> clan name first, then personal name.</li></ul><p>Each race has its own generator on this site with the full naming rules; this page puts them all in one place.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Naming your Tav",
        "content": "<p>\"Tav\" is the name players, and Larian, use for a custom character in Baldur's Gate 3. A few tips for replacing it:</p><ul><li><strong>Keep it short enough to hear often.</strong> Companions say your name a lot. A full dragonborn name is a mouthful; a first name alone is easier.</li><li><strong>Apostrophes and hyphens work</strong>, so githyanki and goblin-style names are fine.</li><li><strong>Think about the companions.</strong> Your name will sit alongside Shadowheart, Astarion, Gale, Karlach, Lae'zel and Wyll in every conversation. Something with its own sound stands out.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Origin characters and their names",
        "content": "<p>The game's origin characters show the naming styles in action. <strong>Lae'zel</strong> is the githyanki pattern exactly. <strong>Karlach</strong> is a tiefling with a short, plain name rather than an infernal or virtue one. <strong>Astarion</strong> is a high elf with a long, elegant name, and <strong>Shadowheart</strong> is a half-elf who goes by a name that isn't really a name at all.</p><p>That last one is a good reminder: a character can go by a nickname, a title or an alias, and the story of why is often more interesting than the name itself.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What races can you play in Baldur's Gate 3?",
        "answer": "Eleven: Human, Elf, Drow, Half-Elf, Half-Orc, Dwarf, Halfling, Gnome, Tiefling, Githyanki and Dragonborn. This generator covers all of them."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a Tav in Baldur's Gate 3?",
        "answer": "\"Tav\" is the name players, and Larian, use for a custom player character, as opposed to playing one of the origin characters like Karlach or Lae'zel."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I change my character's name in BG3?",
        "answer": "You choose it in the character creator. Changing it later isn't part of the normal game, so it's worth picking a name you like before you start."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are these names from the game?",
        "answer": "No. They're new names that follow each race's D&D naming customs, so your character won't share a name with someone in the game."
      }
    ]
  }
